WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.440
A growth cone is the spread fan at the tip of a growing axon.

2
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:14.140
Add brain membranes, and within minutes the fan folds into a thin tip.

3
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:19.860
The protein responsible was purified from chick brain and named collapsin.

4
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:25.580
Today it is called Sema3A, and it works at about 10 picomolar.

5
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:28.520
Retinal growth cones did not respond.

6
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.460
What does the growth cone use to hear it?

7
00:00:33.023 --> 00:00:35.883
Sema3A is a secreted semaphorin.

8
00:00:36.483 --> 00:00:39.723
Two research groups found its receptor, by different routes.

9
00:00:40.403 --> 00:00:44.223
It is called neuropilin, and it binds Sema3A tightly.

10
00:00:45.383 --> 00:00:46.863
But look inside the cell.

11
00:00:47.603 --> 00:00:52.603
Its tail is only about 40 amino acids long, with nothing that looks like an enzyme.

12
00:00:53.663 --> 00:00:57.143
A receptor that catches a signal must also pass it on.

13
00:00:57.943 --> 00:00:58.943
Where is the wire?

14
00:00:59.617 --> 00:01:02.917
The answer was a second protein, plexin.

15
00:01:03.577 --> 00:01:09.237
In engineered cells, neither protein alone let Sema3A shrink the cell.

16
00:01:09.877 --> 00:01:10.977
Together, they did.

17
00:01:11.697 --> 00:01:16.017
And the pair holds Sema3A more tightly than neuropilin alone.

18
00:01:17.537 --> 00:01:19.037
Plexins are a large family.

19
00:01:20.017 --> 00:01:22.557
Membrane semaphorins bind plexins directly.

20
00:01:23.317 --> 00:01:27.577
The secreted class 3 semaphorins reach plexins through neuropilin.

21
00:01:28.367 --> 00:01:30.467
Other work split the jobs.

22
00:01:31.187 --> 00:01:34.647
A neuropilin without its tail still supported collapse.

23
00:01:35.647 --> 00:01:41.247
A plexin stripped of its inner part blocked Sema3A responses in sensory neurons.

24
00:01:42.007 --> 00:01:43.607
So neuropilin catches.

25
00:01:44.467 --> 00:01:45.467
Plexin signals.

26
00:01:46.317 --> 00:01:51.137
Inside the cell, plexin has a domain that acts as a GAP.

27
00:01:51.937 --> 00:01:59.177
A GAP is a protein that speeds GTP hydrolysis, and so switches a small GTPase off.

28
00:02:00.157 --> 00:02:05.017
In the first case studied, Plexin-B1, the reported target is R-Ras;

29
00:02:05.777 --> 00:02:08.097
later work points to its relative Rap.

30
00:02:09.097 --> 00:02:11.857
Both help hold integrin adhesions to the substrate.

31
00:02:12.457 --> 00:02:14.257
Switch it off, and adhesion falls.

32
00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:22.630
Plexin signaling also engages Rho family GTPases and proteins that act on actin and

33
00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:27.650
microtubules. The net result is a local loss of actin and adhesion.

34
00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:31.470
On one side of a growth cone, that means a turn.

35
00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:34.450
Everywhere at once, it means collapse.

36
00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:39.090
Keep the split. Neuropilin catches the semaphorin.

37
00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:40.910
Plexin sends the signal.
